{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"resolve-migration-file-conflict","kind":"prompt","title":"Resolve clashing database migrations","description":"Fixes database migrations from two branches that clash in order or numbering in Django, Rails, Alembic, Prisma, Flyway, Liquibase or EF Core, by merging or renumbering them safely.","category":"git","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["build"],"role":["backend-engineer","software-engineer"],"stack":["git","sql"],"requires":["repo-read","file-write","shell","git"],"inputs":["repo","text"],"output":["diff","report"],"risk":"runs-commands","invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["schema-migrations","migration-ordering","alembic-heads","django-migrations","branch-merge"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["resolve-merge-conflict","review-database-migration"]},"args":[{"name":"context","description":"The framework and migration tool, the error you see (for example \"Multiple head revisions\", \"Conflicting migrations detected\", a duplicate version number), and which environments have already run each side's migrations.","type":"text","required":true}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["The clash","Semantic conflicts","Fix","Check","On next deploy"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nTwo branches that each add a migration often merge without a textual conflict and then break: Django reports conflicting leaf nodes, Alembic reports multiple heads, Rails and Flyway have two migrations whose ordering assumptions differ, Flyway refuses a version lower than one already applied, Prisma's migration history no longer matches. The fix depends on the tool's model: graph-based tools (Django, Alembic) need a merge migration that joins the two leaves, or one branch's migration re-parented onto the other; timestamp or version-ordered tools (Rails, Flyway, Liquibase changelogs, EF Core) need renumbering or reordering so the newer migration really runs after the other. The one thing that decides what is safe is where each migration has already run: a migration applied in a shared or production database must never be edited, renamed or renumbered, because the database records it by name or checksum. Also check for a semantic clash: both migrations altering the same table or column, which merges cleanly but fails or loses data at run time.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<situation>\n{{context}}\n</situation>\n\n1. Identify the tool and read both branches' new migrations in full, plus the tool's current state (`showmigrations`, `alembic heads`, `rails db:migrate:status`, `flyway info`, `prisma migrate status`, the EF Core migrations list).\n2. Ask, if not stated, which environments (developer databases, staging, production) have run each migration. Migrations that ran anywhere shared are frozen.\n3. Check for a semantic clash: same table, column, index or constraint touched by both; dependent data migrations; and ordering assumptions (one adds a column the other's data migration reads).\n4. Fix by the tool's model:\n   - Django: `makemigrations --merge` and review the generated merge migration, or re-point the unapplied migration's dependencies.\n   - Alembic: `alembic merge` the heads, or set the unapplied revision's `down_revision` to the other head.\n   - Rails, Flyway, Liquibase, EF Core: renumber, re-timestamp or regenerate only the migration that has not run anywhere shared, so it runs last; regenerate model snapshots where the tool keeps them (EF Core, Prisma). A conflicted Rails `schema.rb` or `structure.sql` is regenerated by running the merged migrations, not hand-merged.\n   - Prisma: resolve schema.prisma, then create a new migration from the merged schema rather than editing history.\n5. Run the migrations from scratch on a disposable database and, if the tool supports it, from the current state of a copy of the target branch's schema. Run the tests.\n6. Report which files changed and what each environment will do on next deploy.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never modify, rename or renumber a migration that has run on a shared or production database.\n- Never run migrations against a shared or production database; use a local or disposable one.\n- Do not merge migrations by hand-editing generated state files when the tool can generate the merge.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The clash\nTool, the two migrations, where each has run.\n## Semantic conflicts\nShared tables or ordering assumptions, or \"None\".\n## Fix\nFiles changed and commands run.\n## Check\nMigration run results from scratch and from the target state, and tests.\n## On next deploy\nWhat each environment will run, in order.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
